atypical cells present;
Cytologic preparations show cystic material with scant cellularity and rare groups of histiocytes. However, FNA flush specimen sent for thyroglobulin quantification shows isignificantly increased value (165 ng/ml) and is highly indicative of metastatic 
papillary thyroid carcinoma.

Follicular lesion of undetermined significance/atypia of undetermined significance; 

Hypercellular smears with abundant colloid and rare tightly packed clusters of poorly visualized follicular cells, some with a  microfollicular arrangement, consistent with a follicular lesion of undetermined significance (FLUS). No nuclear atypia is 
identified.

negative for malignant cells; 

"LL nodule FNA -
Benign follicular cells, macrophages and colloid present, consistent with a colloid nodule.

Follicular lesion of undetermined significance/atypia of undetermined significance;

"Abundant colloid and scattered follicular cells present. Rare microfollicles are also identified. In view of these findings this lesion most likely represents a hyperplastic colloid goiter nodule. However based on the clinical presentation a follicular 
neoplasm cannot be excluded. Recommend clinical follow-up with histologic evaluation "

Atypical cells present, suspicious for papillary thyroid carcinoma; 

Aspirate sample is cellular and predominantly shows benign follicular cells, macrophages and colloid. Several clusters of atypical follicular cells are noted with nuclear enlargement, chromatin clearing, nuclear grooves and occasional intranuclear 
inclusions. While similar cytologic atypia of follicular cells  can be seen in association of cystic degeneration and repair, in this specimen the multifocality and degree of atypia

